<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baekje Historic Areas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Baekje Historic Areas are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, listed in 2015. They group eight sites from the later capitals of Baekje at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongju">Gongju</a>, Buyeo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iksan">Iksan</a>, including Gongsanseong fortress, the Songsan-ri tombs with the tomb of King Muryeong, Busosanseong, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeongnimsa">Jeongnimsa</a> temple site, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neungsan-ri">Neungsan-ri tombs</a>, the Wanggung-ri palace site and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mireuksa">Mireuksa</a>. Together they show the kingdom's architecture, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and exchange with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> between 475 and 660.</p></div>
